    SAYHI is my second door written for the OzMetro BBS, and it's only
    taken some 24 hours to get it to release stage!  There's not a lot
    of detail a user needs to know, but enough information is provided
    internally for them, and you, to fully understand what SAYHI does.
    What I'll write here are brief notes explaining what SAYHI expects
    of you to get it running on any OzMetro BBS.

    SAYHI doesn't run standalone as yet, and there's probably no need!
    But if someone requires this, I'll have to remedy that for a later
    release!  However, SAYHI should run on all Transamiga BBS systems,
    and probably on a number of others too.  So give it a go, as SAYHI
    just may work on your setup too!


    GETTING STARTED

    This door is set up on OzMetro the same as for any door, and needs
    NO other files to run.  All you need do is place the 'door.exe' in
    a 'door' directory, and then configure a keypress on the menus for
    it.  Yep, as simple as that!  See the OzMetro documentation on how
    to set up doors if you need further information.  If you've set up
    a door or 2 on OzMetro already then you shouldn't have any problem
    at all setting this one up! 8^)  

    You could even install this one as a LOG-OFF door I'd imagine!


    OPTIONAL CONFIG

    A 'SAYHI.cfg' file can also be installed in the same directory as
    the 'door.exe' which then allows you to adjust some of the default
    settings used for SAYHI!  An example 'SAYHI.cfg' has been enclosed
    for you within the archive.  The comments appended to each line of
    the config file can be left there for reference too;  SAYHI strips
    away any comments appended to any line of the config as long as it
    is AFTER a semi-colon!  If any lines are commented out completely,
    or left blank, or else a value entered is outside the given range,
    defaults will be used by SAYHI in all cases.

    Additionally, any lines added at the bottom of the config file are
    completely ignored so you can add anything you like underneath the
    config itself!  Make sure, however, if you DO install 'SAYHI.cfg',
    that EACH setting is found on the precise line the program expects
    it to be on.  For example, (LINE 4) is the ONLY line that will be
    checked for the number of entries SAYHI will add to the 'SayHi.dat'
    data file, before the older entries start to scroll off.


    CONFIG SETTINGS

    The following is a line by line explanation of the config file as
    used by SAYHI. (Versions: 0.01-0.08)

    The setting on LINE 1: this sets the minimum user level which will
    be able to READ any messages left by previous users.  Its range is
    from 1-9 as per the OzMetro user levels, and defaults to 1.  Users
    with an access level less than the value you put on this line will
    NOT be able to access the READ functions of SAYHI at all.

    The setting on LINE 2: this sets the minimum user level which will
    be able to WRITE messages saying 'Hi!' to other users.  Its range
    is from 1-9, as per OzMetro user levels, and defaults to 2.  Users
    with an access level less than the value you put on this line will
    NOT be able to access the WRITE functions of SAYHI at all.

    The setting on LINE 3: this sets the maximum number of lines users
    will be able to add to the 'SayHi.dat' file each day.  The default
    is for 5 lines, but all values in the range from 1-50 can be used!
    (Of course, they can't add any lines if their access level is less
    than the value you have set for write access on LINE 2!)

    The setting on LINE 4: this is the maximum number of entries SAYHI
    is able to add to the 'SayHi.dat' file before the entries start to
    scroll off.  The default is for 20 lines, with values in the range
    from 5-500 being accepted.  There is NO header in the data file as
    SAYHI produces this internally, but allow for an extra 4 lines for
    the header when SAYHI displays the list of messages to the screen!

    The setting on LINE 5: There is NO line 5, anything written on, or
    below this line, is completely ignored by SAYHI.


    EXAMPLE CONFIG

    1  ;LINE1-minimum level required for READ access. (def.1) (rg.1-9)
    2  ;LINE2-minimum level required for WRITE access. (def.2) (rg.1-9)
    3  ;LINE3-number of lines a user can add per day. (def.5) (rg.1-50)
    15 ;LINE4-maximum number of entries: SayHi file. (def.20) (rg.5-500)
    ;=====anything on this line or below is ignored


    FILES CREATED

    SAYHI will create some files in its directory.  Examples of these
    are contained in the archive.  The 'SayHi.dat' file which is where 
    any users messages are stored (in ASCII) has been mentioned above.
    Whenever SAYHI updates this file, it will leave you a copy of the
    old file (renamed as 'SayHi.dat.old') in the same directory.

    A 'SayHi.quota' file is also produced by SAYHI.  This file stores
    the name of any users who have written messages, together with the
    date, and number of messages posted by them, on any given day.  If
    this file is updated by SAYHI a copy of the old file, (renamed as
    'SayHi.dat.old') is left in SAYHI's directory.  Also, this file is
    self-maintaining as any entries created prior to the date when the
    file is updated, are removed during processing by SAYHI!

    Another file SAYHI may create is the 'SayHi.err' file.  There is a
    sample of this in the archive, but hopefully your version of SAYHI
    won't find any reason to use this file at all!  8^)


    DIRECTORIES CREATED

    None!


    IMPORTANT NOTES

    Whenever a user attempts to enter a 1-Line message, their line is
    first checked by SAYHI before it is added to the 'SayHi.dat' file.
    Just like the alias checking in GMINER, SAYHI has an inbuilt check
    for several RUDE words just in case any user decides to run amuck!
    I won't tell you what they are here, but you might like to test if
    the checks are extensive enough once you get it running.

    If SAYHI does miss something, you can always edit your 'SayHi.dat'
    file and remove the offensive material.  Be careful though, if you
    add in, or extend, any lines, make sure they are no longer than 78
    characters, or you'll mess up SAYHI's display!
    

